  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎬 Horror Movie Title Generator",
      "tagline": "Titles for hauntings, slashers, folk horror, creature features, possession stories and found footage, with a tagline to go on the poster."
    },
    "intro": "Horror titles do a lot in very few words: one strange word, or the name of a place where something went wrong. This **horror movie title generator** makes titles in six subgenres for scripts, stories, student films and party games, with a tagline if you want one.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How horror titles work",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>One word:</strong> a single strange word or place name is the classic horror title. It tells you nothing and promises everything.</li><li><strong>\"The X of Y\":</strong> a title with a name in it (The Undoing of Clara Hale) makes it personal, and suits hauntings and possession stories.</li><li><strong>A tagline</strong> does the selling on the poster: short, a little cryptic, and usually a warning.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The subgenres",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Slasher:</strong> the killer with a mask and a body count. John Carpenter's <em>Halloween</em> (1978) set most of the rules.</li><li><strong>Folk horror:</strong> old customs, isolated villages and the land itself. The term was popularised by Mark Gatiss's BBC series <em>A History of Horror</em> (2010), which named <em>Witchfinder General</em>, <em>The Blood on Satan's Claw</em> and <em>The Wicker Man</em> as its \"unholy trinity\".</li><li><strong>Found footage:</strong> the film is the evidence. <em>The Blair Witch Project</em> (1999) made it famous.</li><li><strong>Haunting, possession and creature features</strong> each have their own words: houses and names for hauntings, the body and the soul for possession, and teeth and water for creatures.</li></ul><p>The generator never rebuilds a real film title. Search a title before you use it for a real film, though, as there are a lot of horror films out there. For the cast, try the <a href=\"villain-name-generator.html\">Villain</a>, <a href=\"monster-name-generator.html\">Monster</a> and <a href=\"ghost-name-generator.html\">Ghost</a> name generators.</p><p>For a horror game in a closed-down pizzeria, try the <a href=\"animatronic-name-generator.html\">Animatronic Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What makes a good horror film title?",
        "answer": "Short and strange. One unsettling word, or a place or person something terrible happened to, then a tagline to do the scaring."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is folk horror?",
        "answer": "Horror rooted in old customs, the countryside and the land: isolated villages, strange rituals and the past refusing to stay buried."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these titles for my film?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use. Search the title first in case a film already has it."
      }
    ]
  }
